1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:07,184
A web server will not start on port 8888, and nothing in its config is wrong. The reason:

2
00:00:07,204 --> 00:00:13,605
SELinux gives ports labels too, and a service may only use the ports with its label.

3
00:00:13,625 --> 00:00:16,892
Today we manage those labels with semanage.

4
00:00:17,362 --> 00:00:20,350
In this video: manage SELinux port labels.

5
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:27,453
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:27,923 --> 00:00:29,972
Manage SELinux port labels.

7
00:00:30,442 --> 00:00:33,250
We'll list the port labels, add a port to a label,

8
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:36,587
change a port that already has one, and remove our changes.

9
00:00:44,107 --> 00:00:47,221
Every network service runs with a type.

10
00:00:47,241 --> 00:00:53,568
The web server runs as httpd t, and SELinux lets it use only the ports with the

11
00:00:53,588 --> 00:00:56,862
type http port t: for example 80 and 443.

12
00:00:56,882 --> 00:01:02,165
Port 8888 is not in that list, so the web server is blocked there,

13
00:01:02,185 --> 00:01:05,058
even when the config says to use it.

14
00:01:10,528 --> 00:01:12,557
The tool is semanage port.

15
00:01:12,577 --> 00:01:17,522
Dash l lists, dash a adds, dash m modifies, and dash d deletes.

16
00:01:17,542 --> 00:01:22,646
Dash t gives the type, and dash p gives the protocol: tcp or udp.

17
00:01:22,666 --> 00:01:26,508
The changes are permanent: they survive a reboot.

18
00:01:34,028 --> 00:01:36,392
First, list the labels.

19
00:01:36,412 --> 00:01:43,544
semanage port with dash l prints all of them, so we filter with grep.

20
00:01:43,564 --> 00:01:52,147
The type http port t has the tcp ports 80, 81, 443, 488, 8008, 8009, 8443 and 9000,

21
00:01:52,167 --> 00:01:59,506
and the udp ports 80 and 443. The option dash w means: whole word only.

22
00:01:59,526 --> 00:02:04,999
Port 8080 belongs to another type: http cache port t.

23
00:02:09,519 --> 00:02:17,260
Now add port 8888 to the web labels. Dash a adds. Dash t is the type, http port t.

24
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:24,170
Dash p is the protocol, tcp. And the port, 8888. No output means success.

25
00:02:24,190 --> 00:02:31,079
The list now shows 8888. And dash capital C shows only the local changes:

26
00:02:31,099 --> 00:02:32,688
the ones we made.

27
00:02:37,208 --> 00:02:43,326
What if the port already has a label? Port 8080 belongs to http cache port t.

28
00:02:43,346 --> 00:02:49,066
We try to add it, and semanage says: already defined, modifying instead.

29
00:02:49,086 --> 00:02:55,125
So adding works too, in this version. The option dash m modifies explicitly.

30
00:02:55,145 --> 00:02:58,393
And now port 8080 belongs to http port t.

31
00:03:02,913 --> 00:03:08,682
Last, remove our changes with dash d. First 8888, then 8080.

32
00:03:08,702 --> 00:03:15,919
The local list is now empty. And port 8080 is back with its original label:

33
00:03:15,939 --> 00:03:17,656
http cache port t.

34
00:03:22,176 --> 00:03:26,977
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot when the task needs one, and your turn.

35
00:03:28,497 --> 00:03:32,727
Your turn. Label tcp port 8899 as http port t.

36
00:03:32,747 --> 00:03:40,210
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again: the label must survive the reboot.

37
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:52,431
Try before you open the solution.

38
00:03:57,951 --> 00:04:02,475
Next up: use Boolean settings to modify system SELinux settings.

39
00:04:02,945 --> 00:04:05,848
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
